    <nmo:Mint rdf:about="http://nomisma.org/id/schoonvorst_de">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="http://nomisma.org/id/"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="nl">Schoonvorst</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Schoonvorst</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="de">Schoonvorst</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Schoonvorst, (German: Schönforst) mint of the lords of Schoonvorst and Sichem of the House of Schönau, now situated in the south-eastern district of Aachen-Forst in the municipality of Aachen, Germany, where coins were minted by Reinhard I (1347–1361) and his eldest son Reinhard II (1369–1396). Reinhard II was forced to cede Schoonvorst to the Duke of Gulik in 1396. The latter pledged it to his nephew John I of Heinsberg (1395–1438), after which it passed to Gerard II, Duke of Gulik-Berg (1437–1475). Both of these latter figures also minted coins at Schoonvorst. From 1372 onwards, Reinhard II’s large coins, like those of neighbouring Aachen, bear a date in Roman numerals, making them, apart from the coins of Roskilde (Denmark) from 1234, the oldest coins in Western Europe dated according to the Christian calendar. The known dates on the Schoonvorst coins are: 1372, 1390, 1391, 1392, 1393 and 1394. The oldest dated coins from Aachen bear the dates 1372, 1373, 1374, 1375, 1402 and later. The manor of Sichem (modern Zichem, present-day municipality of Scherpenheuvel-Zichem in the province of Flemish Brabant) appears in the titles on the dated gros of Reinhard II. Conrad I of Schoonvorst, the youngest brother of Reinhard II, inherited Elsloo from his mother in 1368. His son Conrad II of Schoonvorst, Lord of Elsloo (1403–1448), minted coins at Elsloo. His name appears on these coins as KOS DE SCOVOERS or COR(S) DES(C)O(N)VORT.</skos:definition>
        <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://wiki.muntenenpapiergeld.nl/index.php?title=Muntplaatsen"/>
